!! Message 1 ~ 27,655 characters

````
## 1. Diaspora* "aspects"

**Origin — primary sources, verbatim**

- **diaspora* developer release blog post, 15 Sept 2010** — `http://blog.joindiaspora.com/2010/09/15/developer-release.html` (Wayback capture **2010-11-27**, `https://web.archive.org/web/20101127021833/http://blog.joindiaspora.com/2010/09/15/developer-release.html`):
  > "We live our real lives in context, speaking from whatever aspect of ourselves that those around us know. Social tools should work the same way. Getting the source into the hands of developers is our first experiment in making a simple and functional tool for contextual sharing."
  > "Share status messages and photos privately and in near real time with your friends through 'aspects'. … Manage friends using 'aspects'"

- **Private alpha post, 23 Nov 2010** — Wayback capture **2010-11-25**, `https://web.archive.org/web/20101125104227/http://blog.joindiaspora.com/2010/11/23/private-alpha-released.html`:
  > "Diaspora lets you create 'aspects,' which are personal lists that let you group people according to the roles they play in your life. We think that aspects are a simple, straightforward, lightweight way to make it really clear who is receiving your posts and who you are receiving posts from."

- **Priority claim vs Google+ Circles, 8 Sept 2011** — Wayback capture **2011-10-02**, `https://web.archive.org/web/20111002033716/http://blog.diasporafoundation.org/2011/09/08/we-are-making-a-difference.html`:
  > "We're proud that Google+ imitated one of our core features, aspects, with their circles."

So: aspects shipped **2010-09-15** (source) / **2010-11-23** (hosted alpha); Google+ Circles launched June 2011. Predates Circles by ~9 months. **Confidence: high.**

**Do aspects still exist?** Yes. `https://diasporafoundation.org/` (fetched 2026-09-04) still leads with them:
> "diaspora* pioneered the concept of aspects, which means you can organise your contacts according to their role in your life."

Current `develop` branch still has `app/models/aspect.rb`, `AspectsController`, `AspectMembership`, and in `app/models/user.rb`:
- `seed_aspects` creates four defaults: `aspects.seed.family`, `.friends`, `.work`, `.acquaintances`
- `aspects_from_ids(aspect_ids)` — `if aspect_ids == "all" || aspect_ids == :all then self.aspects` (the literal "All aspects" path)
- `post_default_aspects` returns `["public"]` when the user's `post_default_public` flag is set, else aspects flagged `post_default`.

GitHub issue history on aspects runs 2010→2021 (202 matching issues), so the feature has been maintained, not deprecated. **Confidence: high.**

**Semantics (current FAQ, `https://wiki.diasporafoundation.org/FAQ_for_users`, fetched 2026-09-04), verbatim:**
> "Aspects are the way you group your contacts on diaspora*. An aspect is one of the faces you show to the world."
> "Do my contacts know which aspects I have put them in? — **No. They cannot see the name of the aspect under any circumstances.**"
> "Are aspects like 'groups'? — **No. Sharing in diaspora\* is asymmetrical.** … For an aspect to work as a group, each member would have to be connected to every other member and to have placed every other member into one aspect. Group functionality is not something that diaspora*'s software supports at the moment"
> "Can I restrict the posts I see to just those from certain aspects? — Yes." (so aspects are *both* sender-side audience and reader-side filter)
> "Once I have posted something, can I change the aspect(s) that can see it? — No."
> "What happens when I deselect one or more aspects when making a public post? — Deselecting aspects does not affect a public post."

**Measured usage of aspects ("All aspects" vs specific) — NONE FOUND. This is a negative finding.**
- Diaspora's own telemetry is opt-in and *only* counts users/posts/comments. `app/presenters/node_info_presenter.rb` (develop) exposes exactly `total`, `active_halfyear`, `active_month`, `local_posts`, `local_comments`, gated on `AppConfig.privacy.statistics.user_counts? / post_counts? / comment_counts?`. **There is no aspect-usage field, no public/limited split, no "all aspects" counter anywhere in the statistics surface.**
- The project's own post explaining the statistics feature (4 June 2014, `https://blog.diasporafoundation.org/2-how-many-users-are-there-in-the-diaspora-network`, Wayback capture **2014-07-22**) confirms the scope: *"we enabled pods to share statistics about their user base… the most we can say in answer to the question 'how many persons are using diaspora*' is: 'at least this many'."*
- Academic: the one well-known Diaspora measurement paper is **Bielenberg, Helm, Gentilucci, Stefanescu, Zhang, "The growth of Diaspora — A decentralized online social network in the wild," IEEE INFOCOM Workshops, March 2012, DOI 10.1109/INFCOMW.2012.6193476** (109 citations per Semantic Scholar). It is **closed access** — Unpaywall reports `is_oa: false`, Semantic Scholar's abstract field is elided by the publisher. **I could not read it: NOT REACHED.** I therefore cannot say whether it touches aspect usage; from title and venue it is a growth/topology measurement, not a feature-usage study, but I am not asserting that.
- Crossref and Semantic Scholar searches for Diaspora aspect-usage studies returned nothing on topic (Semantic Scholar hit a 429 on one query). **Google Scholar/OpenAlex/WebSearch unavailable per instructions.**
- **Conclusion: I found no measurement, by the project or by researchers, of whether Diaspora users actually posted to specific aspects vs. all. Confidence high that none is publicly available; medium that none exists at all.**

**Closest thing to evidence** is anecdote. HN comment by `dredmorbius`, **2021-11-18**, `https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=29265456`:
> "The problem with G+ Circles (or Diaspora* Aspects, which were an earlier version of the same thing) is that **the people and profiles in your circles have no idea how you've classified them.** … It turns out that what you actually really want are *groups*, not Circles. … The best use I've found for Circles is to group profiles very roughly by interest level, usually into 2–4 tiers… I've used that model on G+, Diaspora*, and Mastodon, pretty effectively."

That is one user, self-reported: the sender-side curation collapsed into a **reader-side volume filter**. **Confidence: low as evidence of population behavior; high that the quote says this.**

**Did it survive? Current numbers.** `the-federation.info` returned **HTTP 502** on 2026-09-04 — **NOT REACHED.** Via `https://api.fediverse.observer/` GraphQL (queried 2026-09-04, `softwarename:"diaspora"`):
- **58 pods listed**
- **sum total_users = 129,502**
- **sum monthly active = 1,675**
- **sum half-year active = 4,817**
- Largest by MAU: diaspora-fr.org (68,541 registered / 379 MAU), diaspora.psyco.fr (11,727 / 354), diasporasocial.net (1,316 / 155), diaspora.mifritscher.de (5,699 / 138), iviv.hu (3,943 / 124).

So: alive, maintained (0.9.1.0 released early 2026 per Wikipedia `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diaspora_(social_network)`), but **~1.7k monthly actives network-wide against ~130k registered accounts — roughly 1.3% MAU/registered.** **Confidence: high for the numbers as reported by fediverse.observer; medium for completeness (opt-in reporting, some pods report 0).**

