## 2. Mastodon lists

**What they are — verbatim, `https://docs.joinmastodon.org/entities/List/` (last updated June 30, 2025; fetched 2026-09-04):**
> "**Represents a list of some users that the authenticated user follows.**"

Attributes and version history:
- `id`, `title` — **2.1.0 added** (lists shipped in Mastodon 2.1, Nov 2017)
- `replies_policy` — **3.3.0 added**; `followed` = show replies to any followed user, `list` = show replies to members of the list, `none` = no replies
- `exclusive` — **4.2.0 added**; "Whether members of the list should be removed from the 'Home' feed"

**The follow-graph constraint, verbatim, `https://docs.joinmastodon.org/methods/lists/`:**
> "Add accounts to the given list. **Note that the user must be following these accounts.**"
> 422 error condition: "You are not following a given account ID, or you do not own the list ID, or list/account ID does not exist."

**User-facing description, verbatim, `https://docs.joinmastodon.org/user/network/` (last updated November 27, 2025):**
> "Lists are subsets of your home timeline. You can create a list, give it a name, and add users that you follow to that list. … List timelines contain only posts by members of that list, as well as replies to you or to other members of the list. This can be changed to show replies to all people you follow, or to no one."

So Mastodon lists are **strictly reader-side and strictly a partition of an existing follow set** — a filter over the follow graph, never an addressing mechanism. Lists are private to the owner; there is no way to post *to* a list. **Confidence: high.**

**Complaints / feature pressure (github.com/mastodon/mastodon), all confirmed via GitHub API:**
- **#7876** "Add accounts to lists without having them in your main timeline", opened **2018-06-23**, closed **2023-05-20** as duplicate by trwnh ("Duplicate of #6982 or #16556 or possibly something that could be done with filters"). Body: *"I can only add accounts to lists, when I follow them and they are removed from lists when I unfollow them. I would like to be able to add people to lists without following them."* — 30 comments.
- **#16556** "[Request] Exclusive lists (hiding listed toots from the primary timeline on an opt-in basis)", opened 2021-08-02, closed. Implemented by **PR #22048 "Add exclusive lists"** (opened **2022-12-04**, merged **2023-06-05**), which states: *"Exclusive lists are a mature (3 year old!) Hometown feature"* — i.e. Mastodon adopted it from the Hometown fork after three years downstream.
- **#8208** "add a option to make lists public" — opened **2018-08-15**, **still open** as of 2026-09-04.
- **#18292** "Shared/Collaborative User Lists" — opened 2022-05-03, **still open**.
- **#7692** "Ability to add hashtags to lists" — opened 2018-05-31, **still open**, 57 comments (highest-comment list issue).
- **#22812** "Create Lists based on topic (hashtags) instead of people" — closed.
- **#189** "Suggestion: Implement lists as groups" — 2016-11-22, closed.

**Measured usage data for Mastodon lists: NONE FOUND.** No usage telemetry is published by Mastodon (the API is per-user and authenticated; there is no aggregate list-usage endpoint). arXiv's API returned empty bodies to `curl` on every query I attempted — **arXiv NOT REACHED**, so I cannot rule out a fediverse-measurement paper touching list adoption. **Confidence: high that Mastodon publishes no such data; low-to-medium that no third-party measurement exists, because arXiv was unreachable.**

