## 3. ActivityPub / ActivityStreams 2.0 audience addressing

**The claim is confirmed. AS2 has rich addressing; Mastodon collapses it to four visibility levels and never reads `bto`/`bcc`.**

**AS2 Vocabulary §5.1 Audience Targeting** (`https://www.w3.org/TR/activitystreams-vocabulary/`, W3C Recommendation 23 May 2017), verbatim:
> "Conceptually, every Object has both a Primary and Secondary audience. … The `to`, `cc`, `bto` and `bcc` properties MAY be used within an Object to explicitly identify the Primary and Secondary audiences."
> "When the event source generates the object and specifies values for the `to` and `cc` fields, the intermediary SHOULD redistribute that object with the values of those fields intact… **This is precisely the same model used by the `to` and `cc` fields in email systems.**"
> "There are situations, however, in which disclosing the identity of specific members of the audience may be inappropriate. … an implementation generating an object MAY use the `bto` and `bcc` properties to list entities to whom the object should be privately targeted. When an intermediary receives an object containing these properties, it MUST remove those values prior to redistributing the object."
> "**Audience targeting information included within an Object only describes the intent of the object creator.** With clear exception given to the appropriate handling of `bto` and `bcc`, **this specification leaves it up to implementations to determine how the audience targeting information is used.**"

`bcc` is defined as: *"Identifies one or more Objects that are part of the private secondary audience of this Object."* There is also a separate `audience` property, and §5.1.1 pairs `audience` with `context`.

**ActivityPub** (`https://www.w3.org/TR/activitypub/`, W3C Recommendation 23 Jan 2018):
- §6.1 Client Addressing: *"clients must be aware that the server will only forward new Activities to addressees in the `to`, `bto`, `cc`, `bcc`, and `audience` fields. The Followers Collection and/or the Public Collection are good choices for the default addressing of new Activities."*
- §7.1 Delivery: *"Targets for delivery are determined by checking the ActivityStreams audience targeting; namely, the `to`, `bto`, `cc`, `bcc`, and `audience` fields of the activity. … If a recipient is a Collection or OrderedCollection, then the server MUST dereference the collection (with the user's credentials) and discover inboxes for each item in the collection."* — **this collection-dereferencing is exactly the aspect/circle primitive.**
- §5.6 Public Addressing: the magic collection `https://www.w3.org/ns/activitystreams#Public`; *"Activities addressed to this special URI shall be accessible to all users, without authentication."*
- §B.11 "Not displaying bto and bcc properties": *"since `bto` and `bcc` are only intended to be known/seen by the original author of the object/activity, servers should omit these properties during display as well."*

**Design intent confirmed by the spec's co-editor.** Christine Lemmer-Webber (`paroneayea`) on Hacker News, **2018-04-14**, `https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=16836027`, verbatim:
> "Hi. Co-editor of ActivityPub here. Nothing about ActivityPub's design is Twitter-esque specific. In fact it largely comes from pump.io's design which is much more Facebook-like than Twitter's design. **We also built ActivityPub so that Diaspora could use it. That's the whole reason collections are addressable, in order to be able to handle the design of Diaspora's aspects.**"

That is a direct, first-party statement that AS2/AP's addressable-collection machinery **exists specifically to carry aspects/circles-style audience curation.** **Confidence: high.**

**Mastodon collapses it to four (well, five) levels — verbatim from Mastodon's own spec-compliance doc, `https://docs.joinmastodon.org/spec/activitypub/`, section "Mentions for addressing and notifications":**
> "Based on the presence or exclusion of `Mention` tags, and compared to the explicitly declared audiences in `to` and `cc`, Mastodon will calculate a visibility level for the post. …
> **public**: Public statuses have the `as:Public` magic collection in `to`
> **unlisted**: Unlisted statuses have the `as:Public` magic collection in `cc`
> **private**: Followers-only statuses have an actor's follower collection in `to` or `cc`, but do not include the `as:Public` magic collection
> **limited**: Limited-audience statuses have actors in `to` or `cc`, at least one of which is not `Mention`ed in `tag`
> **direct**: Mentions-only statuses have actors in `to` or `cc`, all of which are `Mention`ed in `tag`"

**`bto`/`bcc` appear ZERO times in Mastodon's entire ActivityPub spec-compliance document** (grep count = 0, fetched 2026-09-04).

**Confirmed in Mastodon source.** `app/lib/activitypub/parser/status_parser.rb` on `main` (fetched 2026-09-04), verbatim:
```ruby
def visibility
  if audience_to.any? { |to| ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.public_collection?(to) }
    :public
  elsif audience_cc.any? { |cc| ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.public_collection?(cc) }
    :unlisted
  elsif audience_to.include?(@options[:followers_collection])
    :private
  else
    :direct
  end
end
...
def audience_to
  as_array(@object['to'] || @json['to']).map { |x| value_or_id(x) }
end

def audience_cc
  as_array(@object['cc'] || @json['cc']).map { |x| value_or_id(x) }
end
```
There is **no `audience_bto`, no `audience_bcc`, and no reading of the `audience` property at all.** `app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb` likewise only uses `audience_to + audience_cc`. **Confidence: high — this is the running code.**

**Community statements that `bto`/`bcc` are effectively unused.** SocialHub (`socialhub.activitypub.rocks`, the SocialCG-liaised forum):
- `nightpool` (Mastodon core contributor), **2023-01-28**, post `https://socialhub.activitypub.rocks/t/like-activity/2925` (post id 12016), verbatim:
  > "`to`, `cc`, `bto`, and `bcc` are primarily instructions to the *delivering* server about which inboxes to send the activity to. Secondarily, they're instructions for how to treat activities in the `sharedInbox`. Third and least their hints for the receiving client about how to display the activity to the user (e.g. 'why did I receive this?'). **For activities not delivered to sharedInbox where the reason for receiving them is patently obvious, it seems like nobody really felt the need to include them.**"
- FEP-8fcf thread (`https://socialhub.activitypub.rocks/t/fep-8fcf-followers-collection-synchronization-across-servers/1172`), **2020-12-19**, search-blurb verbatim: *"Only directly delivering messages to actors' inboxes works with `bto` and `bcc` semantics currently. **`bto` doesn't work with sharedInbox delivery**"* — i.e. the efficiency optimization the whole network runs on is structurally incompatible with blind addressing. **Confidence: high for the quote; medium that this is the decisive cause.**
- `trwnh`, 2022-07-26, thread 114: *"most [servers] permit people to hide who is in their followers/following and circles/aspects collections"* — and 2024-09-18, thread 4534: *"Revive the Multibox endpoint, or some similar mechanism for specifying exact inboxes to deliver to. The `sharedInbox` we ended up with has some pretty egregious shortcomings…"*
- A SocialHub thread exists titled **"Overlapping taxonomies and the `audience` property"** (topic 4229, 2024-05-10) — the community is still, in 2024, unresolved on what `audience` means.

**The W3C AP implementation report** (`https://activitypub.rocks/implementation-report/`, now banner-labeled *"(Of historic interest only)"*): the row `outbox:removes-bto-and-bcc` shows **5 "yes"** out of 13 listed implementations (Bridgy Fed N/A*, distbin **No**, dokieli N/A, go-fed Yes, Kroeg Yes, Mastodon N/A, microblog.pub Yes, PeerTube N/A, places.pub N/A, Pleroma Yes*, Pubstrate Yes, Smilodon N/A, tags.pub N/A). Mastodon is N/A because it does not implement C2S at all. That is the closest thing to a compliance measurement and it is stale and tiny. **Confidence: high for the numbers; the report is explicitly historic.**

**Negative finding: I found no quantitative measurement of `to`/`cc`/`bto`/`bcc`/`audience` usage across live fediverse traffic.** No FEP measures it; the FEP index (`https://codeberg.org/fediverse/fep/raw/branch/main/README.md`, fetched 2026-09-04) contains no FEP on audience-addressing usage — the nearest-named, **FEP-cd47 "Federation-friendly Addressing and Deduplication Use-Cases"** (DRAFT, dateReceived 2024-05-31), is about content-addressing for moderation/portability, **not** audience addressing, despite the title. Relevant adjacent drafts: **FEP-7502 "Limiting visibility to authenticated actors"** (DRAFT, 2023-12-24), **FEP-db0e "Authentication mechanism for non-public groups"** (DRAFT, 2024-05-03), **FEP-8fcf "Followers collection synchronization across servers"** (FINAL, finalized 2022-02-07). **Confidence: high.**

---

## 4. Nostr — relays as curation

**NIP-65 "Relay List Metadata"** (`https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/65.md`, raw fetched 2026-09-04), status `draft` `optional`, verbatim:
> "Defines a replaceable event using `kind:10002` to advertise relays where the user generally **writes** to and relays where the user generally **reads** mentions."
> "When downloading events **from** a user, clients SHOULD use the **write** relays of that user. When downloading events **about** a user, where the user was tagged (mentioned), clients SHOULD use the user's **read** relays."
> "When publishing an event, clients SHOULD: Send the event to the **write** relays of the author; Send the event to all **read** relays of each tagged user; Send the author's `kind:10002` event to all relays the event was published to."
> "Clients SHOULD guide users to keep `kind:10002` lists small (2-4 relays of each category)."

Origin: PR **#218**, opened **2023-02-03**, merged **2023-02-07**, by Mike Dilger. PR body verbatim: *"Gossip shouldn't have to be so complicated. Nostr should scale better. People should be able to find what they want."*

**Is a relay list audience curation? Explicitly no — it is discovery/routing, not access control.** Mike Dilger, "The Outbox Model" (`https://mikedilger.com/gossip-model/`, page states *"This is a rewrite as of 2024-09-26"*), verbatim:
> "The purpose of the model is to make sure you can find the public content of everybody that you follow, and also to make sure that you (and others) can find replies to content that you create. **To be clear, this model does not apply to all the 'other stuff' on nostr. It only applies to public content.**"
> "It is the website model. It is the RSS model. … You post your content to your outbox relays. You find other peoples content on their outbox relays."

**Its own author's critique — the privacy hole, verbatim from the same page:**
> "in order for your client to find and read my content, I can direct (via my relay list) your client to come over to a relay which you have never even heard of before, which you did not configure, and which you have not vetted. **If privacy is a concern to you, then this is a privacy attack vector.** … I recognize this concern. It is real. But there are solutions to this, namely VPNs and Tor. **If someone can come up with a relay model that is fully distributed, and lets you find everything you need to find, and doesn't have this privacy concern, PLEASE SHARE. I was unable to think one up.**"

So the outbox model **inverts audience curation into publisher-side routing**: the *sender* declares where their bytes live, and the *reader's client* is steered there. Curation of who-sees-what is not in NIP-65 at all. **Confidence: high.**

**Where nostr does put audience curation: NIP-51 "Lists"** (`https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/51.md`), status `draft` `optional`, verbatim:
> "This NIP defines lists of things that users can create. Lists can contain references to anything, and these references can be **public** or **private**."
> "Public items in a list are specified in the event `tags` array, while private items are specified in a JSON array that mimics the structure of the event `tags` array, but stringified and **encrypted using the same scheme from NIP-44** (the shared key is computed using the author's public and private key) and stored in the `.content`."

Relevant kinds — note both reader-side and relay-side curation live here:
- `kind:3` Follow list · `kind:10000` Mute list · `kind:10002` Read/write relays (= NIP-65)
- `kind:10006` Blocked relays · `kind:10007` Search relays · `kind:10012` Relay feeds · `kind:10013` Private relays (nip44-encrypted, NIP-37 drafts) · `kind:10050` DM relays (NIP-17)
- **Sets** (multiple per user, `d`-tagged): `kind:30000` **Follow sets** — *"categorized groups of users a client may choose to check out in different circumstances"*; `kind:30002` **Relay sets** — *"user-defined relay groups the user can easily pick and choose from during various operations"*; `kind:30004/30005/30006` Curation sets; `kind:39089` **Starter packs** — *"a named set of profiles to be shared around with the goal of being followed together"*.
- Spec's own framing, verbatim: *"relay sets can be displayed in a dropdown UI to give users the option to switch to which relays they will publish an event or from which relays they will read the replies to an event"* — **that is sender-side audience selection expressed as relay choice.**

**Critique / open tension in the NIPs repo (via GitHub API, 2026-09-04):**
- **#1282 "Relay Type Nomenclature"** (opened **2024-06-02**, **still open**, 42 comments) is the clearest statement that relays *are* being asked to carry audience semantics, and that nostr has no agreed vocabulary for it. Verbatim from the body: *"Public Outbox Relays… Public Inbox Relays… **Private Inbox**: Relays that accept any event from anyone as long as they tag one of the subscribers or the public in general, however only the tagged individual can download their tagged events… **Private Storage**: Relays that accept events from an author and make sure only the author can download them… **Community Manager Relays**: Relays whose read or write access is closed to members of a NIP-29 group…"*
- **#1179 "Follow lists don't scale"** (opened 2024-04-16, **still open**, 29 comments).
- **#1985 "NIP-51: Adds user's preferred Index, Proxy, Broadcasting and Trusted relay lists"** (opened 2025-07-23, still open).
- **#1135 "nip-64: inbox model"** (opened 2024-03-23, still open).
- **#822 "Update NIP-65 add prioritization (by trust) of list guidelines"** and **#805 "NIP-65 Temporarily omit a relay"** — both closed.
- Real audience privacy in nostr went a different route entirely: **NIP-17 sealed gift-wrapped DMs** (PR #686, opened 2023-07-28, closed, 178 comments — the highest-traffic thread in the repo), i.e. **encryption, not addressing.**

**Measured usage of NIP-65 adoption: NONE FOUND** in the sources I could reach. The Outbox Model page itself says, as of its 2024-09-26 rewrite: *"Unfortunately few clients are creating these (at the time of writing), so these cannot be relied on either."* That is an author's impression, not a measurement. **Confidence: high for the quote; no quantitative adoption figure obtained.**

---

## NOT REACHED / blocked

- **`the-federation.info`** — HTTP **502** on 2026-09-04. Diaspora network size taken from `api.fediverse.observer` instead.
- **Bielenberg et al. 2012 (DOI 10.1109/INFCOMW.2012.6193476)** — paywalled; Unpaywall `is_oa: false`; Semantic Scholar abstract elided by publisher. Contents unread.
- **arXiv API** (`export.arxiv.org/api/query`) — returned empty bodies to every `curl` in this session. No arXiv coverage in this report.
- **`wiki.diasporafoundation.org/Aspects`** — 404 (page does not exist; the aspects documentation lives in `FAQ_for_users`, which I did read).
- **GitHub code search API** — 401 (requires auth). I read raw source files directly instead.
- **Semantic Scholar** — one query hit HTTP 429; two others returned nothing on topic.
- **WebSearch / OpenAlex / Google Scholar** — not used, per instructions.

## Cross-cutting negative finding

Across all four systems, **the same measurement gap recurs**: every one ships the curation primitive, and **not one of them publishes, or has had published about it, any data on whether people use it.** Diaspora's own opt-in telemetry is structurally incapable of answering the question (user/post/comment counts only). Mastodon publishes no list-usage aggregate. The W3C AP implementation report covers 13 implementations, is labeled historic, and measures conformance rather than field usage. Nostr's NIP-65 adoption is described only anecdotally by its own author. **Confidence: high.**
````

